6.text:
I stay with the six-foot division and the consistent metrical filling of the verse-final anceps.

The metrical nuclei that are actually pronounced with the normal synalepha *bene esse* > *be·nes·se* are

et ti bi be nes se so li quom si bi sit ma le

et L by position et·ti, ti S, bi S;
be S, nes L by position nes·se, se S;
so L by nature;
li L in the dative *sōlī*, quom L by nature/position;
si S, bi S, sit L by position sit·ma;
ma S in *măle*, le verse-final anceps conventionally filled L.

That is

et tibi | be nes | se so | li quom | si bi sit | ma le
LSS     | SL    | SL   | LL    | SSL    | SL

i.e. in compact foot notation

LSS SL SL LL SSL SL

*et tibi* = L S S, *be nes* = S L, *se so* = S L, *li quom* = L L, *si bi sit* = S S L with *sit* long by position before *m-*, and the closing iamb *ma le* = S L with the final anceps filled L to close the senarius.

*et*, *nes*, *so*, *quom* and *sit* are long by position/nature on both readings; *li* is the long -ī- of *solī* and *ma* is the short *mă-* of *male*. Marking the verse-final anceps as S would be a raw phonetic transcription of *-le* and would break the consistent metrical treatment of position and elision that is used throughout the line. The dactylic re-grouping LSS SLS LSL SSL LS forces *li* short in *soli* and *ma* long by nature and requires the non-standard SLS amphibrach in the second place. The six-foot reading with the anceps filled L is the coherent scan.

